WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.120
A useful conversation moves beyond the rating.

2
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:10.498
In a fictional coaching example, a supervisor notes that two registration entries lacked the agreed access information.

3
00:00:10.698 --> 00:00:12.987
The missing details delayed preparation.

4
00:00:13.187 --> 00:00:17.459
The supervisor asks what happened and listens before choosing a response.

5
00:00:17.659 --> 00:00:23.966
Perhaps instructions were unclear, the system was difficult to use, or the employee needs more practice.

6
00:00:24.166 --> 00:00:29.283
Those are possibilities to investigate, not excuses or established diagnoses.

7
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:34.614
They agree on a concrete next step, the support needed, and a time to check whether it worked.

8
00:00:34.814 --> 00:00:39.699
Recognition should be equally specific: name the contribution and why it helped.

9
00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:44.515
Keep the conversation respectful and focused on work rather than personality.

10
00:00:44.715 --> 00:00:51.042
Training may help a skill gap, but a workshop cannot repair every workload, resource, or conduct problem.

11
00:00:51.242 --> 00:00:57.714
Follow-through matters because a helpful conversation without support can leave the original barrier untouched.

12
00:00:57.914 --> 00:01:04.698
This teaching sequence is a conversation aid, not a universal script or a prescribed response to the assessed case.
